the coronavirus pandemic has fueled  another crisis  an alarming surge in eating disorders  especially among  adolescents the national eating  disorders association reported a more  than  58 percent increase in calls texts and  social media context to its helplines  from adults and adolescents since the  pandemic started  more than a third of those are from  teenagers between just 13  and 17 years old center for discovery  which operates 15  adolescent clinics for eating disorders  reported more than double the demand for  its residential facilities in 2021  compared to 2019.  cbs news chief medical correspondent dr  john lapook  spoke to one of their counselors  elizabeth masoco she's dealt with her  own eating disorder since she was in her  twenties  mosoko told us she hasn't seen an empty  bed at the clinic since the pandemic  started when you are maybe  feeling sad or isolated it's  kind of those moments where it can creep  in and so  i think that you know quarantine has  definitely taken a toll  on people dr john lapook joins us now dr  lapook good morning  so we just heard elizabeth talk about  how isolation is contributing to this to  this trend what else is happening you  know especially during the pandemic  that's leading to this surge well this  pandemic has been so destabilizing for  so many of us right but especially for  people who have mental illnesses and  that includes people with eating  disorders you have increased anxiety  a loss of your normal routine your  regimen of your day  and a sense of being out of control  right i mean  we can't control covid but you can  control have the illusion of control of  what you're  eating until you said it i never thought  of an eating disorder as a mental  illness  yeah yeah yeah well so yeah you know  there's a lot of research into this um  it's a combination probably of  underlying tendencies like anxiety and  depression obsessive  compulsive disorder but also genetic  factors and brain wiring this  tremendous area of of research into this  but it's so important for people to  understand  that it's something that they can do  something about there's so much stigma  with mental illness and that's one of  the reasons why we want to say yes this  is a form of mental illness but  there's lots you can do if you recognize  well for example  early recognition we understand with  adolescents there's family based therapy  now  where it's all together the parents are  agents of change  and it really matters if you if you  diagnose this early  and that's one of the things that was so  interesting for me we spoke to  therapists and psychiatrists and  families and and patients themselves and  there was a 16 year old girl  who um a hundred pounds and five feet  and at the age of 14 she suddenly  started to lose weight um  she went down to 85 pounds she had a  good enough relationship with her mom  that she said mom mama i think there's  something wrong with me  and because of that she was able to get  into therapy family actually residential  and then inpatient outpatient and she  was okay for a while and then you know  what hit  the pandemic yeah and the pandemic  destabilizing we  we saw a graphic before they said what  to be aware of if you're a parent what  do you look out for here  well you want to look out for change in  your body image you know your unhealthy  relationship with food  looking in the body this 16 year old who  when when the pandemic hit  it happened again and she lost another  15 pounds she said she would look in the  mirror and  even though she was 85 pounds she would  say oh i'm fat  so unhealthy relationship with food  unhealthy relationship with your body  maybe they're going up to their room to  eat their meals they're being secretive  she said told me that she would hide her  food uh and then she would binge  and she would you know so she was  wearing baguette clothes at home so she  wasn't with her normal peer group  her parents weren't noticing and finally  they noticed again and they got  her back into therapy and dr lapu  quickly are these new cases the majority  of them or are they people who have  struggled with this who are relapsing i  spoke to a therapist about that he said  it's a combination of both so  in the case of the 16 year old she had  this problem she relapsed in the case of  a 14 year old boy who started  at 13 to lose weight he had been bullied  as a kid he was overweight everybody  teased him so he started to lose weight  well he lost like 30  40 pounds and his parents were thinking  great  but then he went too far and the father  who was so guilty when i spoke with him  he said you know i'm an educated man and  i have a graduate degree and why didn't  i notice this well  you know guess what boys and girls can  both get have eating disorders  and i think just the awareness of it is  so important and  and i i was very grateful to these  families for talking to me and to really  being so honest you're bringing that  awareness dr thank you so much such an  important topic  if you or someone you know needs help  with an eating disorder  you can text or call the national eating  disorders association hotline  at 1-800-931-2237  the association supports both  individuals and families affected by  eating disorders  we'll be right back
 

